\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Rule-based judges and \textsc  {Directed}\xspace  on the main test sets, over all program--question pairs. Cov.: trusted and answered pairs as a share of all pairs (\%). Err.: wrong answers among them (\%), with the count of wrong answers in parentheses. Sonnet- and Haiku-built programs are shown per platform and pooled; Ollama-built programs (OTF-Tools\xspace  R1 only) are shown apart and never pooled with them. Random refusal keeps a random subset of the unchecked answers at \textsc  {Directed}\xspace  's coverage in each cell. Paired 95\% intervals for every difference are in the artifact.}}{7}{table.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{3}{7}{Rule-based judges and \gate on the main test sets, over all program--question pairs. Cov.: trusted and answered pairs as a share of all pairs (\%). Err.: wrong answers among them (\%), with the count of wrong answers in parentheses. Sonnet- and Haiku-built programs are shown per platform and pooled; Ollama-built programs (\otf R1 only) are shown apart and never pooled with them. Random refusal keeps a random subset of the unchecked answers at \gate 's coverage in each cell. Paired 95\% intervals for every difference are in the artifact}{table.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Model-based judges against \textsc  {Directed}\xspace  on Sonnet- and Haiku-built programs (main test sets). Each judge ran on a fixed subset of $n$ answered pairs; coverage here is a share of these answered pairs, so it is higher than in Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:main}. Matched: the judge keeps its most confident answers up to \textsc  {Directed}\xspace  's count in each cell; the expected number of wrong answers is in parentheses. Own verdict: the judge's own trust or decline. $\Delta $: \textsc  {Directed}\xspace  minus the judge, in percentage points, with the paired 95\% interval; $\Delta $ precision $>0$ favors \textsc  {Directed}\xspace  , and $\Delta $ kept $<0$ means the judge's own verdict keeps more correct answers. Failed: unparsable, cut-off or refused replies and ReAct runs out of turns, all counted as declines, over every row the judge saw.}}{7}{table.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:llm}{{4}{7}{Model-based judges against \gate on Sonnet- and Haiku-built programs (main test sets). Each judge ran on a fixed subset of $n$ answered pairs; coverage here is a share of these answered pairs, so it is higher than in Table~\ref {tab:main}. Matched: the judge keeps its most confident answers up to \gate 's count in each cell; the expected number of wrong answers is in parentheses. Own verdict: the judge's own trust or decline. $\Delta $: \gate minus the judge, in percentage points, with the paired 95\% interval; $\Delta $ precision $>0$ favors \gate , and $\Delta $ kept $<0$ means the judge's own verdict keeps more correct answers. Failed: unparsable, cut-off or refused replies and ReAct runs out of turns, all counted as declines, over every row the judge saw}{table.caption.31}{}}
